From: Diogo Pascoal <diogo.pascoal@procimo.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_ppe_offload: allow offload to Realtek rtl8_4 switches

mtk_flow_get_dsa_port() only substitutes *dev with the DSA conduit for tag
protocols the PPE can generate a tag for. Every other protocol takes the
default case, which returns while *dev is still the DSA user netdev.

mtk_flow_set_output_device() then fails its

	if (dev == eth->netdev[0]) ... else return -EOPNOTSUPP;

check, because a user netdev is none of the GDM netdevs. So a flow whose
egress is a DSA user port on a non-MediaTek switch is refused outright.

On the GL.iNet GL-MT5000 (Brume 3) the LAN ports sit behind a Realtek
RTL8371C using DSA_TAG_PROTO_RTL8_4, while the WAN is the SoC's own PHY on
eth1. LAN->WAN egresses on eth1 and offloads fine; WAN->LAN egresses on
lan1/lan2 and is refused. A flowtable entry needs both directions, so
hardware offload degrades to the software path: ~2.25 Gbit/s with one CPU
core pinned at 100%, versus 2.37 Gbit/s at near-idle on the vendor firmware.

The PPE cannot synthesise the Realtek proprietary 0x8899 CPU tag, so there
is no port-directed offload available here. But port-directed delivery is
not required: rtk_cpu_tagPort_set() programs CPU_TAG_AWARE_CTRL, and the
switch parses an inbound CPU tag only when the 0x8899 Length/Type is
actually present. An untagged frame injected by the PPE on the conduit
therefore takes the switch's ordinary FDB path and is delivered correctly.

Add a case that substitutes the conduit and then returns -ENODEV, so the
caller resolves a real GDM pse_port, skips mtk_foe_entry_set_dsa() and the
DSA queue map, and falls back to queue = pse_port - 1.

mtk_flow_offload_replace() also calls mtk_flow_get_dsa_port(&idev, NULL)
purely for the conduit substitution, so ingress ppe_index selection becomes
correct too. Note this changes nothing on MT7987: ppe_num is 2 and PPE index
follows the MAC id, so the conduit gmac0 maps to ppe_idx 0 - which is already
the ppe_index default that mtk_eth_setup_tc_block_cb() passes in. It matters
only where the conduit is not gmac0.

Note for VLAN setups: an untagged injected frame takes the CPU port's PVID.
Flows egressing a VLAN upper (e.g. br-lan.20) still carry their user VLAN
push, so they land in the right VLAN; flows on the untagged default VLAN
are unaffected. A tagged trunk with no user VLAN push is the case to watch.

Signed-off-by: Diogo Pascoal <diogo.pascoal@procimo.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe_offload.c
@@ -203,6 +203,28 @@
 		if (push_vid)
 			*push_vid = dsa_tag_8021q_standalone_vid(dp);
 		break;
+	case DSA_TAG_PROTO_RTL8_4:
+		/*
+		 * The Realtek 0x8899 CPU tag has no representation in a PPE
+		 * foe entry: the PPE can push an 802.1Q header but cannot
+		 * synthesise a proprietary tag, so no port-directed offload
+		 * is possible.  Still substitute the conduit, then report
+		 * "not a taggable DSA port" so the caller skips both
+		 * mtk_foe_entry_set_dsa() and the DSA queue map.  The frame
+		 * leaves the CPU port untagged and the switch delivers it by
+		 * ordinary FDB lookup - the RTL8371C parses an inbound CPU
+		 * tag only when the 0x8899 Length/Type is actually present.
+		 *
+		 * Without this, the default case returned while *dev was
+		 * still the DSA user netdev, which matches none of
+		 * eth->netdev[] in mtk_flow_set_output_device(), so that
+		 * function returned -EOPNOTSUPP and the WAN->LAN half of
+		 * every flow was refused.  A flowtable entry needs both
+		 * directions, so hardware offload silently degraded to the
+		 * software path on GL.iNet MT5000 (RTL8371C) boards.
+		 */
+		*dev = dsa_port_to_conduit(dp);
+		return -ENODEV;
 	default:
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
